{
  "dom_id": "step-q124533",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "What is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{5\\pi }{9})$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?",
  "worked_solution": "We wish to express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{5\\pi }{9})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ where ${\\theta }_{R}$ is the reference angle corresponding to the angle $\\theta =-\\frac{5\\pi }{9}.$\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{5\\pi }{9})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the corresponding coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$ We do this by adding integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get our desired angle:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5\\pi }{9}+2\\pi =\\frac{13\\pi }{9}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{5\\pi }{9})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{13\\pi }{9}).\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266661/assets/1ee62cca432467a6e025e83dc7e1b2b91e1bdff14a5417030fa04ae8b1c1dac9.png)\n\n- **Step 2**: Compute the reference angle. Since $\\theta =\\frac{13\\pi }{9}$ is in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle is\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{13\\pi }{9}-\\pi =\\frac{4\\pi }{9}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3**: Determine the correct sign. The cosine function is negative $(-)$ in the third quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{5\\pi }{9})=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{9})\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{5\\pi }{9})=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{9}).\n$$",
